            <div xml:id="d208">

                <pb facs="0304" n="230" xml:id="pg230"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 861.00/651</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Morris</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-11-12T17:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 12, 1917, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received November 13, 3.16
                        a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>977. I have been able to get into telephone communication with Torneå and I am
                    informed that according to report current there Kerensky is in control of
                    Russian telegraphs; that telegrams from southern Russia report country is
                    controlled by Cossacks working under Kerensky; that the Bolshevik troops badly
                    beaten at Tsarskoe Selo and retired to Petrograd where they are quartered in
                    certain section of the city; that the. Allied missions in Petrograd are now in
                    connection with Kerensky who has announced Government centers at Moscow,
                    Novocherkassk; that Generals Kornilov and Kaledin have called upon every one to
                    oppose Bolsheviks. According to passengers arriving at Torneå to-day from
                    Petrograd, newspapers are reappearing in Petrograd all announcing defeat of
                    Bolsheviks; that there has been considerable fighting on Nevski Prospekt.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Morris</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
